{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-348109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-348109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{3}-4(2)=8-8=0 \\\\ y & =2(2)^{2}+1=2(4)+1=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,9).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{3{t}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{4(2)}{3(2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3(4)-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,9)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-9 & =x \\\\ y & =x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-348109" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-348109" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-348109" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-6857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}-5,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-6857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=2-3=-1, \\\\ y & =3(1)^{2}-5=3-5=-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{6{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1}{6\\cdot (1)^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6-3} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =2(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-6857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-6857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-6857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y+2=(x+1)"] ["math" "y+2=(x+2)"] ["math" "y+2=2(x+1)"] ["math" "y+2=2(x+2)"] ["math" "y=\\frac{24}{11}\\,x+\\frac{2}{11}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-6857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y+2=2(x+1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-15564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-15564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}=1+0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1=0+1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(\\frac{4}{3},1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-0} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{4}{3},1)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ -1 & =x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)$ is the $x$-intercept." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-15564" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-15564" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-15564" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)"] ["math" "(\\frac{1}{4},\\,0)"] ["math" "(\\frac{2}{5},\\,0)"] ["math" "(\\frac{2}{7},\\,0)"] ["math" "(\\frac{2}{9},\\,0)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-15564" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t}+1,\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3\\cdot 0}+1=1+1=2, \\\\ y & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)),\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))}{3{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}{3{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{3{e}^{2\\cdot 0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,0)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ is the $y$-intercept." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-122698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})"] ["math" "(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{6})"] ["math" "(0,-\\pi )"] ["math" "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122698" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-348115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-348115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)=3e \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3e,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,e} & =\\frac{1}{e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3e,1)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4e}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1=\\frac{1}{4e}(x-3e).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{1}{4e}(0-3e) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{1}{4}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-348115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-348115" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-348115" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-348115" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-348115" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-21081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+10,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-21081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-21081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-21081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-21081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ only"] ["text" "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$"] ["text" "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ only"] ["text" "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$"] ["text" "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-21081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t)=-1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{3\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{7\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350368" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-350368" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350368" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350368" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350368" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7\\pi}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-357255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t),\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-357255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)),\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(t) & =1 \\\\ t & =\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})]=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & ={e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})]=-\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\frac{5\\pi }{4}}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-357255" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-357255" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-357255" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-357255" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-357255" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\pi}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-20853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2(t+1),\\,y=4{t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-20853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=8t.\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n8t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that ${x}^{′}(0)=2\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t=0,$ the tangent to the curve is horizontal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-20853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-20853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-20853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=-1"] ["math" "t=-2"] ["math" "t=0"] ["math" "t=1"] ["math" "t=3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-20853" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the tangent to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is horizontal if ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+14t+8 & =0 \\\\ (3t+2)(t+4) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-4) & =2(-4)+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{15}{2}\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(-\\frac{2}{3}) & =2(-\\frac{2}{3})+\\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{6}\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the tangent to the curve is indeed horizontal at $t=-4$ and $t=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350359" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-350359" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350359" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350359" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350359" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{2}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-6858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=2-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-6858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={0}^{3}-3\\cdot 0=0-0=0, \\\\ y & =2-0=2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {0}^{2}-3} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{0-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{1}{3})\\,}=-3\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-3(x-0) \\\\ y & =-3x+2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-6858" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-6858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-6858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-3x+2"] ["math" "y=-3x+\\frac{12}{5}"] ["math" "y=-\\,\\frac{12}{5}x+2"] ["math" "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+2"] ["math" "y=-\\,\\frac{9}{4}x+\\frac{8}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-6858" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-3x+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-75288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{4}-3{t}^{2},\\,y=2{t}^{3}-t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-75288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(1)^{4}-3(1)^{2}=-2, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(-2,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4{t}^{3}-6t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-1}{4{t}^{3}-6t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{2}-1}{4(1)^{3}-6(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{5}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\,-\\frac{5}{2}\\,)}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=\\frac{2}{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-75288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-75288" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-75288" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)-1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x-2)+1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)+1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{5}{2}(x+2)-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-75288" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{2}{5}(x+2)+1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-15570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,y=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\pi ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-15570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(-1)\\,}=1\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-15570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-15570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-15570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=2x+1"] ["math" "y=2x+3"] ["math" "y=x"] ["math" "y=x+1"] ["math" "y=x+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-15570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=x+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-3t\\,,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=1$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({1}^{2})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡1=0, \\\\ y & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $(0,-1).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{2}{t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}-3}{\\frac{2}{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{3}-3t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$  the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6(1)^{3}-3(1)}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{\\,(\\frac{3}{2})\\,}=-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,-1)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-122898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2}{3}x+1"] ["math" "y=\\frac{4}{3}x+2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to determine the equation of the normal at the point $(1,-2).$ For this, we need to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope ${m}_{n}$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m:$\n\n$$\n{m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the equation of the normal, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,-2)$ and the slope ${m}_{n}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal intersects the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+2 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) & =2 \\\\ x-1 & =-4 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the normal to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis at $(-3,\\,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350386" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-350386" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350386" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350386" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350386" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+8,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})-5,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Do not round the answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =-4\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ and $t=\\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-350414" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350414" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5\\pi}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350414" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350414" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11\\pi}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-21207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{2t},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-21207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The normal line is vertical when the tangent to the curve is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\,⟹\\,t=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(0)=2{e}^{0}=2\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-21207" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-21207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-21207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=0"] ["math" "t=1"] ["math" "t=2"] ["math" "t=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-21207" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=7(t-1),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the points at which the normal to the curve is vertical." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122946" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-122946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "t=\\pm 3\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "t=\\pm \\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "t=\\pm \\sqrt{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122946" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}-8t,\\,y=2{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe normal to the curve is horizontal at $t=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t-8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t-8 & =0 \\\\ 2t & =8 \\\\ t & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(4) & =6{(4)}^{2}=96\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-350403" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-350403" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi t)-2t,\\,y={e}^{2t},\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}-2,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{t}-2=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\frac{1}{2})=2{e}^{2\\times (1/2)}=2e\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122947" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "17f52f129ad3cbfa125fd51c72f1a762fab68af2abbaf9787e31b2daa9f7c6fc"} {"question" "q-122947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=-\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "t=-\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "t=\\frac{3}{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "q-122947" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/state.json" "file_sha256" "8a4aceb17597a5bc0040c11d9044096f0d0467583dbf5f1ae3d9985805d71bd1"} {"question" "e-597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/example-597.json" "file_sha256" "2610ccf49e995cb0c7ee1208e79d5ef2f0eddbc51d36fd6a42ddd805d3d63293"} {"question" "e-597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/assets/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/example-597.json" "file_sha256" "2610ccf49e995cb0c7ee1208e79d5ef2f0eddbc51d36fd6a42ddd805d3d63293"} {"question" "e-6240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty <     t <    \\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/example-6240.json" "file_sha256" "6b16180d10f3937f6e96b177ad83ec4e467e7599f084d293a29fb41a1da6bace"} {"question" "e-6240" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0 .$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1 .\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $y'(0) = -1\\neq 0.$ Therefore, we conclude that when $t = 0,$ the tangent to the curve is vertical." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/example-6240.json" "file_sha256" "6b16180d10f3937f6e96b177ad83ec4e467e7599f084d293a29fb41a1da6bace"} {"question" "e-598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/assets/9dcc4c97bd296ef4627b02b6cfafcdb643f475cd8dacd36a820db760bd6fb045.png)\n\nThe following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/example-598.json" "file_sha256" "5e41fcf73e0989ab4afce9a2de0c2c47f322c7d04498c418cc80fb1eaa109aee"} {"question" "e-598" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1, \\\\  \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point $\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right).$ For this, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ which means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ the slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2}+4(0)}{2(0-\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form \n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1),\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2,\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2) & =-2(x-(\\sqrt{2}+1)) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+2 & =-2x+2\\sqrt{2}+2 \\\\ y+2x & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/example-598.json" "file_sha256" "5e41fcf73e0989ab4afce9a2de0c2c47f322c7d04498c418cc80fb1eaa109aee"} {"question" "e-6252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A curve is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/example-6252.json" "file_sha256" "a7532dbf14bb889d76e9210e29c833fad5a80a83627cb3828c59452dd4090218"} {"question" "e-6252" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while $y'(t) \\neq 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(0) & =2\\cdot 0-1=-1\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043931/example-6252.json" "file_sha256" "a7532dbf14bb889d76e9210e29c833fad5a80a83627cb3828c59452dd4090218"}] "usage" {"e-6252" [] "q-6857" [] "e-6240" [] "q-15570" [] "q-21207" [] "e-597" [] "e-598" [] "q-21081" []} "basis" 1724 "retractions" [[17592186088214 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/799/Source/Images/q-6857.png)\n\nA curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 2t^{3} - 3t, y = 3t^{2} - 5, - \\infty  < t < \\infty $.\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t = 1$."] [17592186088009 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = 4\\sin t + 7, y = 4\\cos t + 10, 0 \\le t < 2\\pi $.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical."] [17592186087960 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = \\ln (\\sin t), y = \\frac{1}{\\sin t}, t \\in  (0, \\pi )$.\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t = \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Find the equation of the normal to the curve at $C$."] [17592186088011 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = e^{2t}, y = t^{2}, 0 \\le t \\le 2$.\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is vertical."] [17592186087520 :question/problem "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty$.\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1$?"] [17592186087520 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}x & = 2 ( - 1 ) + 3 = 1 , \\\\ y & = ( - 1 {)}^{3} - 4 ( - 1 ) = - 1 + 4 = 3.\\end{array}$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3)$. The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/799/Source/Images/f8cfd5f61b0ad19f503ddb05927c280e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nTo compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1$, the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}m = {\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} |}_{t = - 1} & = \\frac{3 ( - 1 {)}^{2} - 4}{2} = - \\frac{1}{2} .\\end{array}$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)\n$$\n\nand the slope\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right)\n$$"] [17592186087529 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t^2+3, \\quad y=-t+1, \\qquad -\\infty < t < \\infty$.\nDetermine the value of $t$ at which the tangent to the curve is vertical."] [17592186087529 :question/worked-solution "The slope $m$ of a tangent line to a curve defined parametrically is given by\n\n$$\nm = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line is vertical if $x'(t)=0$ while\n\n$$\ny'(t) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -1\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n2t=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t=0\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n$$\ny'(0) = -1\\neq 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that when $t = 0$, the tangent to the curve is vertical."] [17592186087521 :question/problem "The following parametric equations define the curve shown above:\n$x=2\\cos{t} +\\sin{2t},\\quad y=\\cos{t} - 2\\sin{2t},\\qquad 0\\leq t<2\\pi$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/799/Source/Images/b6d3cface8e385a6ebaa2fe18a25c564.png)\n\nCalculate the equation of the normal to the curve at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (shown as a dashed line in the plot above)."] [17592186087521 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\ninto the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= 2\\cos (\\frac{\\pi }{4}) + \\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\sin (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} + 1, \\\\\ny &= \\cos (\\frac{\\pi }{4}) - 2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2\\sin (\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2(1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the normal at the point\n\n$$\n\\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right)\n$$\n\nFor this, we need to find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwhich means we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$, as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = -2\\sin t + 2\\cos 2t, \\qquad y'(t) = -\\sin t - 4 \\cos 2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} & = \\frac{{y}^{\\prime } ( t )}{{x}^{\\prime } ( t )} \\\\  & = \\frac{- \\sin   t - 4 \\cos   2 t}{- 2 \\sin   t + 2 \\cos   2 t} \\\\  & = - \\frac{\\sin   t + 4 \\cos   2 t}{2 ( \\cos   2 t - \\sin   t )} .\\end{array}$$\n\nAt the point where\n\n$$\nt=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nthe slope $m$ of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}m & = {\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} |}_{t = \\pi  / 4} \\\\  & = - \\frac{\\sin   ( \\frac{\\pi }{4} ) + 4 \\cos   ( \\frac{\\pi }{2} )}{2 ( \\cos   ( \\frac{\\pi }{2} ) - \\sin   ( \\frac{\\pi }{4} ) )} \\\\  & = - \\frac{\\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{2} + 4 ( 0 )}{2 ( 0 - \\frac{1}{2} \\sqrt{2} )} \\\\  & = \\frac{1}{2} .\\end{array}$$\n\nTo find the slope $m_n$ of the normal line, we take the negative reciprocal of $m$:\n\n$$\nm_n = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = -\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)} = -2\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the normal line, we use the point-slope form\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m_n(x-x_1)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = \\left(\\sqrt{2}+1,\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} -2\\right), \\qquad m_n=-2\n$$\n\nand we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 2) &= -2(x - (\\sqrt{2} + 1)) \\\\\ny - \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 2 &= -2x + 2\\sqrt{2} + 2 \\\\\ny + 2x &= \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087530 :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x=2t^3-1,\\quad y=t^2-t+1,\\qquad-\\infty < t <\\infty$.\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the **normal** to the curve is horizontal."] [17592186087530 :question/worked-solution "The normal is horizontal if the tangent is vertical. Therefore, we require $x'(t)=0$ while\n\n$$\ny'(t) \\neq 0\n$$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx^{\\prime }(t) = 6t^{2}, y^{\\prime }(t) = 2t - 1\n$$\n\nSolving $x'(t) = 0$ gives\n\n$$\n6t^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad t = 0\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{\\prime }(0) &= 2 \\cdot 0 - 1 =-1 \\ne 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t = 0$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186084772 17592186084773 17592186084774 17592186084775 17592186084776 17592186085058 17592186085059 17592186085060 17592186085061 17592186085062 17592186085070 17592186085071 17592186085072 17592186085073 17592186085074 17592186086216 17592186086217 17592186086218 17592186086219 17592186086220] "immutable_fields" [[17592186084771 :answer-field/correct] [17592186084771 :answer-field/id] [17592186084771 :answer-field/key] [17592186084771 :answer-field/type] [17592186085057 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085057 :answer-field/id] [17592186085057 :answer-field/key] [17592186085057 :answer-field/type] [17592186085069 :answer-field/correct] [17592186085069 :answer-field/id] [17592186085069 :answer-field/key] [17592186085069 :answer-field/type] [17592186086215 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086215 :answer-field/id] [17592186086215 :answer-field/key] [17592186086215 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
